## 1. Introduction
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted enormous attention owing to their unique advantages such as ease of fabrication and excellent optoelectronic performance.[1–3] The hole transporting materials (HTMs) are recognized as key materials in
porting molecule (SAHTM) containing an anchored group has been established to construct high-performance p–i–n structured PSCs.[7–11] Such self-assembled molecules offer multiple benefits over conventional HTMs, including reduced material consumption, tunable bandgap, and simple processability. From a structural perspective, these de novo SAHTMs consist of two parts with different functionalities.[12] One is the anchoring group, such as carboxylic acid, which chemically bonds the molecules to the surface of the transparent conductive oxides substrate. Another is electroactive aromatics (e.g., carbazole, phenothiazine) modifying the photophysical properties of SAHTMs. Benefiting from the tunability of groups, such modular structure allows for rational design and synthesis to choose the functionalities required in a specific application.[13] The donor–acceptor \((D{-}A)\) type groups with excellent electron-donating and hole-transporting properties, such as triarylamine and related materials-based groups, are ideal for constructing high-performance self-assembled hole-transporting molecules.[14–18]
In 2018, Getautis et al. first employed self-assembled molecules in p–i–n PSCs as the hole-selective contact and obtained
a PCE of \(17.8\%\).[19] Further optimization of the SAHTMs and processing conditions have been reported thereafter. Guo et al. employed \(D{-}A\) -type molecules as HTMs for high-performance PSCs and obtained a PCE of \(21.24\%\).[20] Deng et al. introduced a co-assembled monolayer as the hole-selective contact, exhibiting a PCE of \(23.59\%\) , which is one of the highest reported efficiencies to date.[21] Moreover, SAHTMs have also been successfully applied in perovskite mini-modules and perovskite-silicon tandem solar cells.[22–24] Although these research works have made great contributions to the application of SAHTMs in the field of PSCs, they are not sufficient to achieve the goal of efficient and stable PSCs.[25,26] Most of the current \(D{-}A\) -type SAHTMs have carboxyl groups as the anchoring groups. Carboxylic acids, particularly \(\alpha,\) \(\beta\) -unsaturated carboxylic acids, undergo decarboxylation processes under thermal conditions.[27] Few SAHTM-based devices have been observed to have satisfactory operational stability, which severely hinders the application of these SAHTMs (Scheme S1).
In this work, we design two \(D{-}A\) -type self-assembled molecules with an anchoring group of phosphonic acid as HTMs for p–i–n PSCs. As illustrated in Figure 1a, 4-methoxy-N-(4- methoxyphenyl)N-phenylaniline and triphenylamine are introduced as donor groups while the benzo[c][1,2,5]thiadiazol (BT) serves as the acceptor unit. Michaelis-Becker conversion of BT halides to corresponding phosphonates and subsequent hydrolysis yield these novel phosphonic \(D{-}A\) SAHTMs – PPA and PPAOMe.[28] Compared to the commonly used carboxylic groups in SAHTMs, phosphonic acid is a more suitable anchoring group with superior stability owing to its dianionic nature to form predominantly bidentate bonding with inorganic metal oxides on the indium doped tin oxide (ITO) substrates, improving the coverage and ordering of phosphonic acid analogs.[29–35] In addition, density functional theory (DFT) results show that PPA and PPAOMe interact with ITO through sulfur and oxygen, forming a preferred "face-on" molecular orientation and lowering the hole injection barrier at the anode/HTL interface.[34] Through the modulation of the donor group, PPA has a suitable energy level and high hole extraction capability. Besides, PPA could passivate the perovskite buried interface by interaction with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) , enhancing interfacial stability. As a result, we achieved stable efficiency \({>}23\%\) with an open-circuit voltage \(\left(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\right)\) of \(1.14\mathrm{~V},\) a short-circuit current density \(\left(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\right)\) of \(24.83\mathrm{~mA~cm}^{-2}\) , and a fill fact (FF) of \(82\%\) . More importantly, under the thermal condition \((85\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , the PPA-based device maintained \(87\%\) of the initial PCE after being aged for \(1000~\mathrm{h}\) . Besides, the PPA-based PSC presented outstanding operational stability, retaining \(91\%\) of its original efficiency after maximum power point (MPP) tracking for \(1000~\mathrm{h}\) under 1 sun illumination. The optimized device architecture is transferable from labscale prototypes to large-area mini-modules and a champion efficiency of \(19.51\%\) for an active device area above \(17\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) was obtained. These results demonstrate that developing both costeffective and stable SAHTMs is a useful strategy for achieving highly efficient and long-term operating-stable PSCs.
