## 2. Results and Discussion
The synthetic pathway of PPA and PPAOMe is displayed in Experimental Section, involving steps of Suzuki coupling and Michaelis–Becker conversion.[35] The cost of these two molecules was calculated to be as low as \(\\(30\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) compared
to PTAA (Table S1, Supporting Information). The chemical structure and excellent purity of both PPA and PPAOMe were confirmed by NMR spectra and high-resolution mass spectrometry (Experimental section). The molecular geometry was explored using Gaussian 09 and the molecular orbital energy levels were investigated by DFT calculations, as illustrated in Figure 1b. The dihedral angles between BT and triphenylamine in PPA and PPAOMe are \(33.7^{\circ}\) and \(35.3^{\circ}\), respectively, exhibiting good structural configuration in both two \(D{-}A\) SAHTMs. Electrostatic potential (ESP) surfaces of both molecules are also plotted to visualize the electron density distribution. The electron-rich areas in both molecules are predominantly distributed on the sites of the anchoring group, providing favorable conditions for molecular absorption on the ITO surface. Additionally, the anchoring group can also passivate perovskite defects as the Lewis base. According to frontier molecular orbital results, the lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals (LUMOs) of both molecules are concentrated on BT, whereas the highest occupied molecular orbitals (HOMOs) are delocalized on the triphenylamine group. The values of the LUMO energy levels of PPA and PPAOMe are \(-2.82\) and \(-2.70\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively. The HOMO energy level of PPA is \(-5.20\ \mathrm{eV}\) which is better aligned with the energy level of the perovskite material, compared to the \(-4.89\ \mathrm{eV}\) of PPAOMe (Table S2, Supporting Information). Cyclic voltammetry measurements of both SAHTMs also confirmed this electrochemical behavior. The initial oxidation potentials of PPA and PPAOMe are 0.79 and \(0.50\ \mathrm{V}\), respectively (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The corresponding HOMO energy levels are \(-5.3\) and \(-5.0\ \mathrm{eV}\), which agree well
with the calculation results. The electron-donating -MeO group decreases the oxidation potential of PPA, resulting in a \(0.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) difference in the HOMO levels between PPA and PPAOMe.[36] The well-aligned energy level of PPA facilitates the increase of the Voc of the PSC device. In this study, we employed PTAA, a commonly used polymeric HTM, for comparative purposes. The energy level diagrams of the device and SAHTMs are displayed in Figure S2 (Supporting Information).
The interfacial property of ITO/SAHTMs was studied to explore the interaction between the anchoring group and the ITO substrate. The optical absorption properties of the SAHTMs in the film and solution were characterized by UV–vis spectroscopy (Figure S3, Supporting Information). In the solution, both PPA and PPAOMe show two distinct absorption peaks at 430 and \(455\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively. After being deposited on ITO, the absorption peaks of PPA and PPAOMe shift to 475 and \(496\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively, suggesting significant intermolecular \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions (Figure S3C, Supporting Information).[37] Meanwhile, the high transmittance of the ITO/SAHTMs would not affect the light absorption of the perovskite layer. Thermogravimetric analysis was also performed to test the thermal stability of the new molecules (Figure S4, Supporting Information). PPAOMe kept losing weight from the beginning of the heating process, while PPA was heated to \(\approx250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) before weight loss occurred. The morphology of ITO/SAHTMs was then studied by atomic force microscopy (AFM) (Figure 2a–c). The ITO/ PPA film shows a smoother surface with a root-mean-square (RMS) roughness of \(3.31\ \mathrm{nm}\), compared to the RMS roughness of \(4.87\ \mathrm{nm}\) for ITO/PPAOMe film. The lower RMS roughness
of ITO/PPA suggests the formation of higher quality \( D{-}A \) SAHTMs film, demonstrating fewer defects formed on the surface of the PPA layer.[38] We also studied the water contact angles of bare ITO, ITO/PPA, and ITO/ PPAOMe (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The contact angle increases from \( 33.79^{\circ} \) to \( 88.65^{\circ} \) and \( 81.53^{\circ} \), respectively, confirming that both PPA and PPAOMe have been effectively attached to the ITO surface.
Previous studies have shown that the work function \( \left(\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{F}}\right) \) of ITO can be regulated by interfacial modifications.[39] UV-photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurement was conducted to investigate the modulation of the \( \mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{F}} \) of ITO by PPA and PPAOMe (Figure S6, Supporting Information). The \( \mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{F}} \) of bare ITO substrate is \( 4.40\;\;\mathrm{eV} \), which is consistent with previous reports. After being modified with SAHTMs, the \( \mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{F}} \) increases to \( 4.48~\mathrm{eV} \) for PPA, and \( 4.58~\mathrm{eV} \) for PPAOMe, respectively. The effective modulation of the ITO work function is attributed to the large dipole moment of these two molecules. DFT calculation results show dipole moments of 6.97 and \( 8.86\mathrm{~D~} \) for PPA and PPAOMe, respectively, resulting in higher positive charge density near the ITO surface which leads to the formation of electrostatic potential steps. As a result, it becomes more difficult for electrons to move from the Fermi level to the vacuum level, thus increasing the \( \mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{F}} \) of the ITO substrate.[40]
The interaction between PPA and ITO surface was also investigated by DFT simulations. The oxygen atoms in PPA engage with the in atom at the ITO surface, as illustrated in Figure 2d, with a predicted bond length of \( 2.78\mathrm{~\AA~} \). The binding energy between PPA and ITO is calculated to be \( -34.71\;\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}} \). The PPA molecule reaches a stable equilibrium state during interfacial rearrangement, in which the bond length of S-In is calculated to be \( 3.41\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}} \). These results suggest that PPA and the ITO surface are packed closer together with a preferred face-on orientation. Such tighter packing between PPA and the ITO substrate, beneficial for effective hole extraction, is attributed to the rigid and planar structure of PPA, as well as the strong interaction. The packing information of PPA was probed using 2D grazing-incidence wide-angle scattering (GIWAXS). There are no noticeable scattering peaks observed along the \( q_{\mathrm{xy}} \) direction (Figure S7, Supporting Information). By comparison, relatively broader \( \pi{-}\pi \) stacking scattering signals along the out-of-plane direction are revealed by GIWAXS, confirming the preferred "face-on" orientation. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) also confirmed these interactions. As shown in Figure 2e, two main single peaks (444.16 and \( 451.72\ \mathrm{eV} \)) are observed for ITO and assigned to the In \( 3d_{3/2} \) and \( 3d_{5/2} \) orbitals. After the adsorption of PPA and PPAOMe, both peaks shift to higher binding energy, demonstrating the interaction of PPA and PPAOMe with ITO. Similarly, the Sn peaks show the same trend in the XPS spectra (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Besides, ITO/PPA shows a larger binding energy shift, suggesting that PPA has a stronger interaction with ITO.
Uncoordinated \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) ions at the HTM/perovskite interface serve as the nonradiative recombination center, obstructing charge extraction and transport.[41,42] In PPA and PPAOMe, the oxygen atom's localized negative charge is advantageous for forming bonds with the positive-charged \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) ions, therefore passivating the interfacial defects. To explore the defect passivation effect of PPA and PPAOMe, the chemical state of
Pb was detected by XPS measurement (Figure 2f). The signals of the Pb \( 4f \) orbitals exhibit two main peaks at \( 138.13\ \mathrm{eV} \) \( (4f_{7/2}) \) and \( 142.96\ \mathrm{eV} \) \( (4f_{5/2}) \) for the PTAA sample. The core-level Pb \( 4f \) peaks of the perovskites deposited on PPA and PPAOMe show a \( 0.3\ \mathrm{eV} \) shift to the higher binding energy, indicating stronger chemical bonding between the \( D{-}A \) SAHTMs and the perovskites. Thus, the PPA and PPAOMe could effectively passivate the HTM/perovskite interfacial defects.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was used to investigate the morphologies of the perovskite films deposited on PTAA, PPA, and PPAOMe (Figure 3a–c). The result reveals larger and more uniform grains in the perovskite film deposited on PPA, suggesting a lower density of defects (Figure S9, Supporting Information). All samples show additional bright nanoplates which are regarded as the residual \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \). X-ray diffraction (XRD) further verified this result. As illustrated in Figure 3d, the perovskite films show XRD peaks at \( 14.02^{\circ} \), \( 19.91^{\circ} \) \( 24.43^{\circ} \), \( 28.28^{\circ} \), and \( 31.70^{\circ} \), corresponding to the (100), (110), (111), (200), and (210) crystallographic planes, respectively. The peak of the excess \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) is located at \( 12.8^{\circ} \). However, the perovskite film deposited on PPA shows the lowest intensity for the peak attributed to \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \), indicating that PPA facilitates the crystallization of perovskite.
To verify the defect passivation effect of \( D{-}A \) SAHTMs, we employed the space-charge limited current measurements on a hole-only device with the structure of ITO/SAHTMs/ perovskite/ Spiro-MeOTAD/Au to calculate the trap density (Figure S10, Supporting Information). The trap state density is determined by the trap-filled limit voltage \( (V_{\mathrm{TFL}}) \) using the equation \( 2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}V_{\mathrm{TFL}}/e L^{2} \).[43] Where \( e \) is the elementary charge, \( L \) is the thickness of the perovskite layer, \( \varepsilon \) is the dielectric constant of perovskite, and \( \varepsilon_{0} \) is the vacuum permittivity. The \( V_{\mathrm{TFL}} \) values for the PTAA, PPA, and PPAOMe-based devices are 1.05, 0.59, and \( 0.84\mathrm{~V}, \) respectively. The hole trap densities are calculated to be \( 1.33\,\times\,10^{16}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3} \) (PTAA), \( 7.5\,\times\,10^{15}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3} \) (PPA), and \( 1.06\times10^{16}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3} \) (PPAOMe). This result confirms that the
PPA could effectively suppress the formation of trap states at the HTM/perovskite interface.
To estimate the photovoltaic performance of different SAHTMs, photovoltaic devices were fabricated with a structure of ITO/SAHTMs/perovskite/ PCBM/2,9-dimethyl-4,7-diphenyl-1,10-phenanthroline \((\mathrm{BCP})/\mathrm{Ag}\), where \(\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.85}M A_{0.1}P b I_{3}}\) perovskite was used as an active layer. Figure S11 (Supporting Information) shows the crossed-sectional SEM image of the device, clearly showing the structure of each layer. Figure 4a presents the champion devices' current density–voltage \((J\!\!-\!\!V)\) curves of the PTAA, PPA, and PPAOMe-based PSCs under standard AM 1.5G illumination. Their relevant photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table S4 (Supporting Information). The PTAA-based device exhibits a PCE of \(21.25\%\) with a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.11\;\mathrm{V}\), \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(24.48~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\), and FF of \(78.2\%\). A PCE of \(21.52\%\) with \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.10\ \mathrm{V}\), \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(24.70\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\), and FF of \(79.2\%\) is obtained for the PPAOMe-based device. Meanwhile, the best-performing PPA-based device presents a significant increase in \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and FF, resulting in the best PCE of \(23.24\%\) \((V_{\mathrm{OC}}\colon1.14\ \mathrm{V},\,J_{\mathrm{SC}}\colon24.83\ \mathrm{mA\cm}^{-2}\), and FF: \(82.0\%\) ). The improved \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) and FF are attributed to the suppression of the non-radiative recombination and the efficient hole extraction at the HTM/perovskite interface. Besides, the PPA-based device achieves time-dependent power outputs of \(23.20\%\) after being illuminated for \(200\,\mathrm{~s~}\) at \(0.97~\mathrm{V}\) (Figure 4b), confirming the reliability of the device performance. External quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of the devices are depicted in Figure 4c. The integrated \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) for the PTAA, PPA, and PPAOMe-based
devices are calculated to be 24.01, 24.58, and \(24.36\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\), respectively, consistent with the values obtained from the \(J{-}V\) measurements. The distribution of the PCEs of 20 individual devices based on different SAHTMs is shown in Figure 4d and the devices based on PPA exhibited excellent reproducibility. Then, perovskite solar modules (PSMs) were fabricated comprising six sub-cells in series connection. The best PSM with PPA achieves a high PCE of \(19.51\%\) (aperture area of \(17.11\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)), with a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(6.72\,\mathrm{V}\), \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(3.62\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\), and FF of \(80.2\%\) (Figure S12, Supporting Information).
To further investigate the effects of different SAHTMs on the charge-recombination lifetime \((\tau_{\mathrm{r}})\) and charge-transport time \((\tau_{\mathrm{t}})\) of the PSC devices, transient photovoltage (TPV) and photocurrent (TPC) decay measurements were performed on the complete devices (Figure 4e,f). The \(\tau_{\mathrm{r}}\) of the PPA-based devices \((31.8~\upmu\mathrm{s})\) is substantially longer than that of the PPAOMe and PTAA-based devices (28.4 and \(17.6\,\mathrm{\ns}\)), which is attributed to the passivation effect of PPA leading to suppressed nonradiative recombination. Additionally, \(\tau_{\mathrm{r}}\) of the PPA-based device is 332.9 ns, which is much shorter than that of the reference device based on PTAA \((533.2\,\mathrm{\ns})\), indicating that PPA could effectively extract photogenerated charge carriers in the perovskite layer.[44,45] We further examined the \(J{-}V\) curves of the devices in the dark to determine the leakage current caused by carrier recombination. Compared with the other two devices, the PPA-based device shows a lower saturation current density \((J_{0}=4.7\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2})\), indicating less charge recombination (Figure S13, Supporting Information).[46]
One issue restricting the commercialization of PSCs has been their instability. Therefore, we investigated the stability of the PSC devices under various conditions. For storage stability, the unencapsulated devices were stored in a dark dry box with \( 30{-}50\% \) relative humidity (RH) at room temperature. Compared to the PTAA-based device which maintained \( \approx 47\% \) of its initial PCE after \( 1200\ \mathrm{h} \) storage, the PPA and PPAOMe-based devices retained \( 81\% \) and \( 62\% \) of their initial PCEs, respectively. The aged perovskite films of the above samples are exhibited in the inset of Figure 5a. An obvious degradation is observed for the film deposited on PTAA, showing a prominent \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) peak \( (12.6^{\circ}) \) in the XRD spectra originating from the decomposition of the perovskite crystals. Meanwhile, the PPA-based perovskite film shows minor \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) peaks (Figure S14, Supporting Information). According to the International Summit on Organic Photovoltaic Stability (ISOS)-D-2, unencapsulated devices were examined in the air at a temperature of \( 85\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \).[47] As shown in the photographs (inset of Figure 5b), PTAA and PPAOMe-based devices became yellow after \( 500\ \mathrm{h} \) of the ISOS-D-2 stability test, indicating severe degradation. Their efficiency decreased to less than \( 50\% \) after \( \approx 300\ \mathrm{h} \) of aging. The PCE of the PPA-based PSC device retained \( 86\% \) of its initial efficiency after a rapid initial degradation ("burn-in"). The operational stability (ISOS-L-1) of these devices was also investigated. The MPP tracking of the unencapsulated device was performed under continuous illumination in \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) atmosphere at \( 55\pm5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). The PPA-based device retained over \( 91\% \) of its initial PCE after \( 1000\ \mathrm{h} \) of MPP tracking (Figure 5c). By linear extrapolation,
we estimated that the lifetime of the PPA device could be over \( 2000\ \mathrm{h} \) at \( 80\% \) efficiency \( \left(\mathrm{T}_{80}\right) \), which is one of the longest \( \mathrm{T}_{80} \) values for such devices (Scheme S1, Supporting Information). Previous reports illustrated that the interfacial interactions of the perovskite layers with the HTM are the most critical factor determining device stability.[48] It is, therefore, inferable that the stronger interaction of PPA with ITO and perovskite surface results in the improved stability of the device.
Ion migration or diffusion in PSCs leads to the degradation of the perovskite film and the perovskite/PCBM heterojunctions, as well as corroded metal electrodes.[49,50] To unveil the mechanism of the stability enhancement, the distributions of iodide and silver ions in aged PTAA and PPA-based PSCs were investigated by secondary ion mass spectroscopy (ToF-SIMS) \( (55\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), illumination for \( 500\ \mathrm{h} \)). In the PTAA-based device, a considerable amount of iodine species penetrate into the electron transporting layer and the silver electrode. In the meantime, silver species diffuse into the perovskite layer, resulting in the degradation of the PTAA-based device. The PPA-based device, on the contrary, shows significantly suppressed diffusion of these chemicals (Figure 5d). It has been demonstrated that a high concentration of holes promotes ion migration and shortens charge carrier lifetimes.[51] Therefore, the ToF-SIMS measurement also indicates that the PPA-based PSC device could effectively extract holes, suppress undesirable ion migration, and prevent degradation of the device.
To further investigate the character of the HTM/perovskite buried interfaces, we performed AFM-IR to explore the
chemical components at the PTAA/perovskite and the PPA/ perovskite interfaces.[52,53] To test the micro-nano morphology and the chemical fraction of this buried interface, a transfer process was employed to peel the perovskite film from the HTM layer.[54,55] We characterized the signal at \(1713~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , corresponding to the stretching vibration of the \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{N}\) bond of \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) . Thus, the IR mapping reflects the distribution of \(\mathrm{FA^{+}}\) at the HTM/perovskite interface. As shown in Figures 5e,f, both fresh samples exhibit a uniform distribution. After aging under the ISOS-L-1 condition for \(1000~\mathrm{h}\) , the PTAA/perovskite device illustrates significant inhomogeneity, and the FA signal at the grain boundaries is weaker than that in the interior, indicating the decomposition of perovskite at the interface of PTAA. In contrast, the color of the FA signal in the PPA/perovskite device, which was aged for \(1000~\mathrm{h}\) under the same condition,
remains similar to that of the fresh sample, indicating that the PPA molecules enhance the interfacial stability between the perovskite and HTM films, delaying the decomposition process of the perovskite film. Therefore, it is inferred that the long-term stability of the device is attributed to the fewer trap state density and surface passivation effect of the PPA SAHTM.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, two \(D{-}A\) type self-assembled hole-transporting molecules PPA and PPAOMe with phosphate anchoring groups were synthesized and applied as HTLs in p–i–n PSCs. The phosphonic acid groups interact strongly with the ITO
substrate and the perovskite layer, improving the interfacial contact. The core building block of triphenylamine results in better-aligned energy levels between the ITO substrate and the perovskite layer, enabling efficient hole extraction and electron blocking. Consequently, the PPA-based PSC device achieved a PCE of \(23.24\%\), outperforming the device based on PTAA, which is the conventional molecular HTM. PSMs were fabricated with PPA and a PCE of \(19.51\%\) was obtained (aperture area of \(17.11\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)). The unencapsulated PPA-based device maintained \(86\%\) of the initial efficiency under continuous thermal stress at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(500\,\mathrm{h}\). Meanwhile, after \(1000\,\mathrm{h}\) of continuous MPP tracking, the PPA-based device maintained \(91\%\) of the initial efficiency. This work highlighted that tailored \(D{-}A\) type SAHTMs with phosphonic acid groups have great potential for improving the performance of PSCs, opening new avenues to the commercialization of PSCs.
